#!/usr/bin/env python3
# store — the GOG and Epic stores for RetroForge: sign-in in Chromium on the device, the library, installs,
# and launches of Windows builds through our Proton, with cloud saves. .claude/plans/stores.plan.md.
#
#   store <gog|epic> status            {"ready": bool, "signed_in": bool, "account": "..."} (no network)
#   store <gog|epic> setup             the store's tool, into the player's home; N% lines
#   store <gog|epic> sign-in [--display :N]
#                                      the store's sign-in page in Chromium (SIGN-IN below), on that X
#                                      display (else DISPLAY): JSON events on stdout; closing stdin cancels it
#   store <gog|epic> sign-out
#   store <gog|epic> games             JSON: every game the account owns, installed or not
#   store <gog|epic> art               the listed games' covers into the cards' cache (ART below)
#   store <gog|epic> install <id>      N% lines; exit 0 once installed
#   store <gog|epic> uninstall <id>
#   store <gog|epic> launch <id>       saves down, the game, saves up; the game's exit status
#   store browser status               {"ready": bool, "binary": path|null}: the sign-in's Chromium (no network)
#   store browser setup                that Chromium, into the player's home; N% lines (BROWSER below)
#
# Errors go to stderr as `store: ...`. Exit 0; 1 for a failure (the last stderr line says why); 2 for a
# bad invocation; 3 when the store is not set up or not signed in; 4 when the network cannot be reached.
#
# THE TOOLS. legendary (Epic) and gogdl (GOG) are the stores' own open tools, as Heroic drives them. Each
# is a zipapp for the system python3, pinned to a release and its sha256 (/usr/share/retroforge/stores/).
# `setup` downloads it, checks it, unpacks it into <data>/retroforge/stores/tools/<name>-<tag>/ and
# compiles it there: a zipapp recompiles its own modules on every run, 3.7 s to start on the Air X for
# legendary, 0.7 s unpacked (2026-10-24). Both __main__.py skip their own unpacking outside a zip.
#
# RUNNING A GAME. Windows builds only. The game runs in its own Wine prefix with the Proton
# steam-compat-tools keeps for this CPU (our ARMv8.0 build without LSE atomics, else Proton-CachyOS),
# inside Steam Linux Runtime 4 arm64: Steam's copy when it installed one, else ours, downloaded at the
# first install (steamrt4.pin). The command is the one retroforge-steam-direct replays,
#   <runtime>/_v2-entry-point --verb=waitforexitandrun -- <proton>/proton waitforexitandrun <exe> <args>
# with the STEAM_COMPAT_* variables. STEAM_COMPAT_CLIENT_INSTALL_PATH is an empty folder of ours: Proton
# copies Steam's client DLLs from there only when they exist, and their mtimes are part of its prefix
# check, so a Steam update would otherwise redo every store prefix's set-up.
# The game and its prefix's set-up start at oom_score_adj 900, as Steam's launch wrapper starts a Steam
# game, so either OOM killer takes the game first. GRIP ran at the session's 0 on the Air X and the
# kernel killed the session's audio and bus instead (2026-10-24). This process keeps the session's
# value, to upload the saves after the game.
#
# PROTONFIXES. Our Proton runs umu-protonfixes' per-game fixes (proton-armv8 patch 0003), which find a
# store game by UMU_ID and STORE (gog, egs) in its environment, as umu-run sets them. The umu-database
# maps the store's id to the umu id; it is cached in <data>/umu-database.csv, fetched again when a day
# old, and a game it does not list is umu-default (only the store's defaults run). RetroForge's own
# fixes, for what umu-protonfixes does not cover on ARM, are in <pins>/game-fixes.json: before the game
# runs, each sets an INI key in its prefix. A player turns both off per game in store-games.json
# (GAME_CHOICES): umu-protonfixes' were written for x86 GE-Proton.
#
# SIGN-IN. RetroForge never takes a password. `sign-in` opens the store's own sign-in page in Chromium
# (python/chromium_signin.py; .claude/plans/store-signin-window.plan.md), fullscreen over OGUI, where the
# player signs in by touch and the on-screen keyboard; Google refuses sign-in in an embedded web view, so
# it is the real browser, set up at the first sign-in (BROWSER below). Chromium is driven over its
# DevTools pipe, which opens no port, and the store's code is read where the store ends: GOG's in the
# address it redirects to, Epic's in the JSON page of legendary's client. The window then closes and the
# code signs in. Its profile is a temporary folder, deleted after. Neither store has a device sign-in
# this could use instead: GOG has no device-code grant, and Epic's is open only to its own apps'
# clients. Tokens stay in the player's home, which only the player can read.
#
# BROWSER. The on-device sign-in (.claude/plans/store-signin-window.plan.md) opens the store's page in
# Chromium, which is not on the image: `browser setup` (and `sign-in`, when it is not set up yet)
# downloads the snapshot packages chromium.pin lists, checks each against its sha256 and unpacks them
# into <data>/retroforge/stores/chromium/ (python/chromium_setup.py). No package manager: release roots
# have none.
#
# ART. A Steam card's cover is on disk already (the client's library cache); a store's is on its CDN,
# and a card that fetched its own (library_store.gd: one curl, one TLS handshake each) came in slowly
# as the player scrolled. So once a library is listed, `art` fetches every portrait cover into the
# same cache, on a few kept-alive connections at a low priority: the cards then read them from disk.
# A card shown before then still fetches its own.
#
# TEST SEAMS (tests/test-store.sh, tests/test-store-browser.sh): RETROFORGE_STORE_DATA (for <data>/retroforge/stores),
# RETROFORGE_STORE_PINS, RETROFORGE_STORE_TOOL_<NAME> (a tool's command, instead of the set-up one),
# RETROFORGE_STEAM_ROOT, RETROFORGE_STEAM_COMPAT_TOOLS, RETROFORGE_STORE_RUNTIME (a runtime folder),
# RETROFORGE_STORE_RUN (a command run with the game's command line instead of it), RETROFORGE_STORE_CHROMIUM
# (a browser command instead of the set-up Chromium), RETROFORGE_STORE_SCALE (its scale instead of the
# screen's), RETROFORGE_STORE_API (a server every https:// request goes to instead,
# as <it>/<host>/<path>).
